<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Philopoemen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 253–182 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Philopoemen of Megalopolis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Philopoemen was a general and statesman of Megalopolis who served eight times as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strategos">strategos</a> of the Achaean League. He reformed the league's army on the Macedonian model, defeated the Spartan tyrant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/machanidas">Machanidas</a> at Mantinea in 207 BCE and brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> into the league in 192 BCE. Captured by the rebellious <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/messenians">Messenians</a> in 182 BCE, he was forced to drink poison.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philopoemen.jpg" alt="Philopoemen" width="320" height="271" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mapofphilopoemen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
